EXT. SALT FLAT - HIGH NOON

White. Flat. Shimmering to the horizon. A dead windmill. A single iron water trough, bone dry.

LUCINDA "LUCKY" TREVINO-ASH (30s, sun-cracked lips, black duster, a rifle across her saddle) sits a paint mare in the glare.

Across the flat, a man on foot. BARNABY CROW (60s, one blind eye, a gold tooth, no horse). He drags a coffin lid behind him on a rope.

Lucky waits. He keeps coming.

LUCKY
That's far enough.

BARNABY
Is it?

He keeps coming.

LUCKY
Barnaby.

BARNABY
Lucky.

LUCKY
Where's your horse?

BARNABY
Ate it.

LUCKY
You didn't.

BARNABY
Wanted to.

He stops ten paces off. Sits on the coffin lid. Wipes his face.

BARNABY (CONT'D)
Got any water?

She tosses him a canteen. He drinks. Pours some on his head. Hands it back, empty.

LUCKY
That was my last.

BARNABY
I know.

Her rifle comes up. He doesn't flinch.

BARNABY (CONT'D)
Before you shoot me. Look in the lid.

LUCKY
Why?

BARNABY
Humor an old man.

She keeps the rifle on him. Leans out of the saddle and flips the lid with her boot. Underneath: a bundle of bank notes, wet and rotting, tied with twine.

LUCKY
That the Dunmore money?

BARNABY
What's left. The salt got in.

Lucky laughs. A short, cracked bark. Barnaby laughs, too. They are both still laughing when the rifle goes off.

The shot echoes. Barnaby looks down. Nothing. The bullet has kicked up salt a foot from his boot.

LUCKY
That's for the horse.

BARNABY
(wheezing)
Fair.
